      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city sprang to life when railroads cut through the area, turning Hubbard into a vital rail hub in the late 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hubbard%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1895, mineral water was discovered in Hubbard, leading to bath houses and a sanitarium that drew visitors seeking health cures.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hubbard%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hubbard is the hometown of baseball legend Tris Speaker, who won the Chalmers Award in 1912 while playing for the Cleveland Naps.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hubbard%2C_Texas",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Known as the Victorian Crossroads of Central Texas, Hubbard preserves a collection of historic homes that showcase 19th‑century architecture.",
